; preludes: form-stdlib/json.fk form-stdlib/cache.fk form-stdlib/form-ontology-bp.fk form-stdlib/line-grammar.fk form-stdlib/bmf-core.fk form-stdlib/bmf-grammar.fk form-stdlib/name-lexicon.fk form-stdlib/neutral-symbol-grammar.fk
;
; neutral-symbol-grammar-band.fk — the dictionary emerges from its own grammar.
;
; PROOF LEVEL: FOUR-WAY as of 2026-07-26. **15 on fkwu, go, rust and ts** (re-witnessed 2026-09-03; one run that day saw rust/ts refuse bp "entry" while siblings edited the registry).
;
; It read FOURTH-ARM ONLY until then, for the reason it stated: "the entry
; grammar's cursor walks multibyte zh/ar/sa surfaces — the same cross-kernel
; string-unit seam nl-many-band names ... When cursor unit parity opens, this
; band's roster row is part of the acceptance." It opened. `cur-peek` in
; bmf-core.fk now reads its byte through `str_byte_at` instead of
; `(ord (char_at s p))`, and 首 / رأس / शिरस् survive the walk on every arm.
; Before that, this band crashed on go, rust and ts with `as_nid: null` out of
; nsg-kid — the parse died inside a character and handed back nothing.
; The delimiter-walk renderer in name-lexicon-band (2047 four-way) is unmoved;
; it never crossed the seam, and it still doesn't.
;
; Bits: 1 a known line parses to the right row; 2 a parsed row emits back to
; the identical line; 4 EVERY table row survives emit->parse->row identity
; (all 64 — the whole dictionary emerges from its grammar); 8 the emerged
; count equals the table count exactly. Verdict 15.
(do
    (let line "y7 head 首 رأس शिरस् .")
    (let row (nsg-parse-entry line))

    (let c0 (if (and (str_eq (nth row 0) "y7")
                (and (str_eq (nth row 1) "head")
                     (and (str_eq (nth row 2) "首")
                          (and (str_eq (nth row 3) "رأس") (str_eq (nth row 4) "शिरस्"))))) 1 0))
    (let c1 (if (str_eq (nsg-emit-row row) line) 2 0))
    (let c2 (if (eq (nsg-emerged-count) (nlx-count)) 4 0))
    (let c3 (if (eq (nlx-count) 64) 8 0))

    (add c0 (add c1 (add c2 c3))))
